ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287721 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287721 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287721 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181685" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287721 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287721 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f2/f2d1c647ec7899221567d67e24e90a9f1efca60650b82a739e843e4085be636e.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287721 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2898c964-611b-56ce-8ed9-635cb443e151" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287721 #edb/ref 1074 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=2x$ and *below* the line $y=-x+3.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>2x,\\,y<-x+3.\n$$\n\n \n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n2x<y<-x+3.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the boundary is not included in the set (the lines are dashed), the inequalities are strict.\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,2x<y<-x+3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287724 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287724 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287724 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287724 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181688" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287724 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287724 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/46/4628a6e45445a585361f7d32586b27e29bc726d4ed8b7f072679e5537df59644.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287724 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dab3d6dc-4b31-53f2-8ab3-bc64485c9198" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287724 #edb/ref 1074 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=x+1$ and *below* the line $y=3x.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>x+1,\\,y<3x.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\nx+1<y<3x.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y=3x$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\nx+1<y\\le 3x\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x+1<y\\le 3x}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287781 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287781 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287781 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181976" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287781 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287781 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/11/11ec43034f97e66840da012f3beaf2d933fb03cfbe69f510ca5b68f1fd861f83.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287781 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "827675a7-728a-52ab-b17b-7e94ef593363" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287781 #edb/ref 1074 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-x+1.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-x+1\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is *not* included in the set (the curve is *not* solid),  no further action must be taken, so we have\n\n$$\ny>-x+1.\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>-x+1}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287786 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287786 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287786 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181999" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287786 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287786 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c7/c7f7b1dc9bf4da61b573345aaf92fe0d29ea00ef5558a9fca985d8af25fa0a03.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287786 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "53ac5dfb-f75e-5ef2-a84c-e58da3b6698e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287786 #edb/ref 1074 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>2\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is *not* included in the set (the curve is *not* solid),  no further action must be taken, so we have\n\n$$\ny>2.\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409894 #edb/ref 1080 "16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409894 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409894 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "53607ff6-5913-50ea-923c-e269930ccd31" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409895 #edb/ref 1080 "<" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409895 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409895 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d74e258e-f0dd-579c-9148-3c83bfa32b0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409896 #edb/ref 1080 "9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409896 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409896 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d9099832-9536-5764-b8f9-8